      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles weak/unsalted password hashing: hashlib.sha256(token.encode()).hexdigest() is a plain unsalted single-round hash, which scanners flag as weak credential hashing. It is actually safe because hash_token only hashes opaque high-entropy server-generated tokens (session ids and API keys produced by secrets.token_urlsafe(32)/token_hex), not user passwords. Passwords use bcrypt via passlib CryptContext (hash_password). SHA-256 is the correct, fast, fixed-length one-way digest for high-entropy random tokens, where salting/key-stretching is unnecessary."
      },
